Conclusions

| **Experiment**                      | **Training Accuracy** | **Test Accuracy** | **Train–Test Gap** |
| ----------------------------------- | --------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------ |
| Baseline CNN                        | 99.39%                | 77.46%            | 21.93%             |
| CNN + Data Augmentation (10 epochs) | 89.20%                | **84.06%**        | **5.14%**          |
| CNN + Data Augmentation (20 epochs) | 92.41%                | 83.20%            | 9.21%              |


Baseline
-Excellent training accuracy.
-Much lower test accuracy.
-Large gap → clear overfitting.

Augmentation (10 epochs)
-Lower training accuracy.
-Highest test accuracy.
-Smallest gap.
-Best generalization.

Augmentation (more epochs)
-Training accuracy increased from 89.20% → 92.41%.
-Test accuracy decreased slightly from 84.06% → 83.20%.

This is a classic pattern:

By training longer, the model fit the training data a bit better, but that extra fitting didn't translate into better performance on unseen images.

The drop from 84.06% to 83.20% is only 0.86 percentage points, so it's not dramatic, but it suggests that 10 epochs may have been closer to the sweet spot for this particular setup.

